+# ---- Perf 2 onepos_pipeline (perf_experiments/onepos_pipeline/README.md). On a Tensix core whose
+# walk is ONE position (one tile-row of one column block) read -> tilize -> write run back to back.
+# ONEPOS_SUB_BLOCK_TILES > 0 cuts that tile-row into column sub-blocks of at most that many tiles
+# (never 1 tile): compute pushes each sub-block's tiles (raw WH fast-tilize LLK, the row stride
+# decoupled from the sub-block width: the helper cannot tilize a column slice of a wider row, and a
+# resident input shard's row IS wider) and the writer writes each one at once, in store_rows' tile
+# order (the production order starts at the sub-block holding the rotated first tile; a column-order
+# start put the Tensix cores' first writes on half the DRAM banks: +8.6 % [1,1,32,8192], +13 %
+# [1,1,64,4096]). On a RESIDENT output (the writer RISC-V idle) with the plain stick reader, the
+# reader lands the tile-row as 2 column parts (one transaction id each) instead, while each part's
+# stick segment keeps >= ONEPOS_SPLIT_MIN_PART_BYTES, and compute tilizes part 0 while part 1 lands
+# (256-B parts: +11 % on [1,1,2048,512] DRAM -> HEIGHT_SHARDED). 0 = off (the op's kernels exactly).
+# Measured WH B0, 64 Tensix cores, device-kernel ns, medians of 10 / 6, off -> on:
+#   [1,1,2048,512] HEIGHT_SHARDED L1 -> DRAM   17189 -> 15823 (-7.9 %)
+#   [1,1,2048,512] DRAM -> HEIGHT_SHARDED L1   12105 -> 11859 (-2.0 %)
+#   L1-interleaved source, resident inputs of other widths / dtypes: -4 .. -7 %; DRAM -> DRAM: flat
+ONEPOS_SUB_BLOCK_TILES = 2
+ONEPOS_SPLIT_MIN_PART_BYTES = 512


